3 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "659072cc-8b2c-5e8d-b6a6-88321581f47f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050885 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050885 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050885 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050885 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050885 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050885 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050885 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050885 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429143 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050885 #edb/ref 1031 "Gradient Vector Fields" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050885 #edb/ref 1033 3692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050885 #edb/ref 1034 0.9436080156561308 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050885 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6e3d5713-6e26-51db-8b8f-61d36b28e342" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1031 "The Domain of a Multivariable Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1033 1899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1034 1.1523219435529348 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050992 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "77b779f9-f0f6-53e5-821f-44c6d9f4ce47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179601 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9536" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179601 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d5/d51d0cea4b75dffe5eef6ccf46e5156716eb1381d20b4193d811acfe83278094.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the curve and constant vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$\n\nII. $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =                 0$\n\nIII. $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =  0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179601 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d5a9cb43-6396-5153-a60c-3d653d61a47b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179601 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <                                 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I is false, while statement II is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $\\mathbf F$ works with the particle for $x >        0,$ while $\\mathbf F$  works against the particle for $x <           0.$ \n\n![](images/67/673798bb131da05a07b3f47894340cbbc143fa6a248823bcfbdf6b5a5238efb4.png)\n\n \nBy symmetry, the contribution to $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ for $x <                 0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $x > 0.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =     0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/24/24884d31b2b9aa8d94b11bccfaab1fa31e62a34f4e5183676c79390923f1f243.png)\n\n \nSo, $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =      0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179604 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9542" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179604 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/29/298b7b4293ff104a62107670fa2aba603b8a23e9ad042f7e270a4acf6267a2fa.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <  0$\n\nII. $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =         0$\n\nIII. $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r >  0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179604 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d844a573-49a1-59c3-bec4-e409c486469d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179604 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <                     0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/cd/cd71f379a02405bc21aecfb0bb14e9798cfbc4d7fd2c1d3a1d5221aa8d64638f.png)\n\n \nSo, $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/b2/b2284e7ffa962fd46333c4eca47a77473007fdd96e0813b9928f0303df8170f3.png)\n\n \nSo, $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179739 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179739 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179739 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179739 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179739 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179739 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179739 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179739 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179739 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179739 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179739 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179739 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186