502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179848 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179848 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179848 #edb/ref 1045 "Interpreting Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179848 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186180497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179848 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179848 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179848 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ff0789e0-f094-5fc5-ac25-230d7c590b14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180019 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460603 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186180019 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460604 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186180019 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460605 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186180019 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125380" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180019 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180019 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/14/145b9dcc1e52dec65d64245afa89fba77e0d0cda5a919b52009e2397f3f03e16.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186180019 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "58d69dfd-31b1-5851-b7cd-9ea022ec672e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180019 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/20/20121d30445c9e756f1c2c024713bc6cd6f0464cfcfc24df68bc30c766c4988e.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/0f/0ff98536c275458d68deabda91f56bf63e7c8f59f5ea79c68292332d8cd58d4f.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180493 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180493 #edb/ref 1061 35727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180493 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0c7bbec7-4f05-52c6-86b2-ee55d5eec5cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180493 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180497 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180497 #edb/ref 1061 35733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180497 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2c33a62f-70ce-5226-b2d4-39e1df8540da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180497 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180498 #edb/ref 1061 35747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180498 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e76a04e3-a2f3-5a0a-9547-3a509b4d4d5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180498 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180502 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180502 #edb/ref 1061 35802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180502 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dda3ff66-7674-5b97-99fb-85a94a2b066a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180502 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180503 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180503 #edb/ref 1061 35803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180503 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "51bd906d-35f0-5627-ad2d-ac5987f58545" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180503 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180720 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ and $C$ is a smooth curve in the $xy$-plane. Recall that the line integral\n\n$$\nW = \\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r\n$$\n\nrepresents the total work done by $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along $C.$ Loosely speaking:\n\n- if $W > 0,$ then $\\mathbf F$ causes the particle to *speed up* as it moves along $C,$ and\n\n- if $W < 0,$ then $\\mathbf F$ causes the particle to *slow down* as it moves along $C.$\n\nTo understand this, consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the path $C$ defined by the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ shown below. \n\n![](images/2a/2a3b3896767e5f6beb4eec39f44afcc2387e178df0cec0ebbbdaa5e4cb68e964.png)\n\n \n\nIt's helpful to imagine that $\\mathbf F$ represents a uniform gravitational field near the earth's surface. \n\nLet's consider two examples.\n\n- If a particle moves from $A$ to $B$ along the segment, it will pick up speed because it moves *with* the vector field:\n\n![](images/0f/0f0bbc24f7ed26390643546bf578b88a879da58f56ac0b817159deeea25f164b.png)\n\n \nIntuitively, this makes sense. We know from real-world experience that if we drop a pebble, its speed increases as it descends. \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW = \\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0.\n$$\n\n- If a particle moves from $B$ to $A$ along the segment, it will slow down because it moves *against* the vector field:\n\n![](images/2e/2eabe985ee8b360ef79841561547b2251a818e63e53e91785a83d0c2cf3609e3.png)\n\nAgain, this matches our intuition: If we toss a pebble into the air, its speed decreases as it ascends.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW = \\int\\limits_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r < 0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180720 #edb/ref 1065 5834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180720 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2e08c55c-9b68-57ce-97fe-81922244739a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180720 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180723 #edb/ref 1064 "In some situations, a particle's speed increases and then decreases (or vice-versa) as it moves along a curve in a vector field. \n\nLet's consider a particle moving from $A$ to $B$ along the arc of a semicircle centered at $O$ in a uniform gravitational field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/46/466bd1716e626f6e3cc7859589974c27d49bdaa77bed9aa5813ef4bd9f3328e9.png)\n\n \n\nNote that:\n\n- as the particle moves from $A$ to $T,$ it works against $\\mathbf F$ and slows down, and\n\n- as the particle moves from $T$ to $B,$ it works with $\\mathbf F$ and speeds up.\n\nBy symmetry, the contribution to $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ for $x <                   0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $x > 0.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =     0.\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this means that the particle's speed is the same at the points $A$ and $B,$ and these are the points of maximum speed. \n\nAdditionally, since $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ is decreasing as we move from $A$ to $T,$ the particle moves at its slowest speed at the point $T.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180723 #edb/ref 1065 5843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180723 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f659a6f3-f2aa-5170-a3d7-e5ba24fd59c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180723 #edb/ref 1067 "Cases When a Particle Experiences Acceleration and Deceleration" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180724 #edb/ref 1064 "Another noteworthy situation is when ![](images/c7/c75d4b37611dd632920692d430870b4d582fed06ac19178ccf69eb7c7dcb264a.svg) is perpendicular to ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) at all points along ![](images/27/278f99df1e14fe0ebfb61bdb72262a62670dcaa60d322148bc3ee591b278ec2d.svg)\n\n![](images/f3/f3f8b96e06c9e802e038130db69356924df9c9a385dc913e55cb5d9cc3a697b1.png)\n\n \n\nSince the force ![](images/c7/c75d4b37611dd632920692d430870b4d582fed06ac19178ccf69eb7c7dcb264a.svg) acts perpendicular to the particle's motion as it moves from ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) to ![](images/65/65ada9c919fe5c4bee19fb4cafff28ed46a36bf6fc90b4cecf70e0fa02386ce0.svg) it neither helps nor hinders its motion. Therefore,\n\n![](images/06/06b0f05fc417c4572bb7763607ef1d471efc1282067b22c372f23caba33fcb19.svg)\n\nIn particular, if ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) is a level curve of a scalar field ![](images/bc/bc562d54615dad55d095db2afed7626f62ff7ebf0ab6c5d45471b77ac30b6398.svg) and ![](images/c5/c5b03af80a954012283edf6f027c02c7fe387d08ee6424e6b92186d580a65e7e.svg) then\n![](images/b3/b3dddb4aa6f178a9ab5ec8245e4881b260d60cfba33123b56b8b5ea99cda95d8.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180724 #edb/ref 1065 5844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180724 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "13e79581-4a65-5db7-9362-f065ba606386" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180724 #edb/ref 1067 "Cases When the Vector Field and Curve are Perpendicular" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259814 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462847 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186259814 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462848 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186259814 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462849 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186259814 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125491" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259814 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259814 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f0/f0daaf51b1aab267c11b8457b0e7e1480ff5aabd3acaa15a07f54625c6d8103d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186259814 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "35e675be-a5b1-5d4c-9024-2339a508b912" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259814 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/17/1764bfc51a25475162a70bad7239a36f5d099aa6157c71ed7ed3e02e474eb906.png)\n\n \n\nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/05/059731f0cac61f19382172192c6a9f7f366ecb758767797f9bc8311faa3865af.png)\n\n \n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259816 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462853 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186259816 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462854 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186259816 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462855 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186259816 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125495" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259816 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218625981